    "content" : "Imagine you went to sleep with a strap around your head. The strap contained sensors that read your brain patterns/visual memory, and was connected to an iPad-like device on your nightstand. <b>When you wake up in the morning, there&#39;s a video of your dreams, recorded on the iPad.</b> Sound like complete and utter fantasy? From the breakthroughs made by these scientists, this sounds like it could become a reality in our lifetime... <br /><br />If it does ever happen, I&#39;m calling it now: it&#39;ll be <b>a new art form</b>, or at the very least, cool fodder for viral videos on YouTube. Who will be the coolest &quot;dream artist&quot; on YouTube of 2025? (Though this article doesn&#39;t mention it, I&#39;m guessing it could also one day have sound.) <br /><br /><b>Update: A different story on controlling your dreams, from Scientific American</b>: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/293hg6l\" class=\"ot-anchor\">http://tinyurl.com/293hg6l</a>",
